OK — L10n Guard: PASS

Two implementation defects found and fixed while building this:

(a) `grep -c -Fxf -f "$tmp_lines" ...` -- the combined `-f` consumed the *next*
    `-f` as its filename, so the pattern file was the literal string `-f` and
    every match count was 0 (the run reported "0 of 20 Chinese lines reappear").
    Caught by comparing against the standalone python measurement in §2.
    Now `grep -c -Fx -f`.
(b) staleness was judged with `exists_at "$AFTER"`, so a ref-vs-itself
    comparison called every allow-list entry stale -- which is exactly what the
    CI step I had just written (`--before HEAD --after HEAD`) printed:
        FAIL — stale allow-list entries:
        crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/src/views/usage_detail.rs(path is back at HEAD; delete this entry)
    Staleness is now a property of the working tree, not of the compared range:
    an entry whose path is in the working tree records a removal that did not
    happen. That also catches a file restored *later*, outside the range being
    compared -- which the ref-based version missed.

