7. SIDE FINDING: the doc checker reported link syntax that was never a claim
================================================================================
Widening `check-doc-l10n.py --links` to the glob CI never runs (`sync/**/*.md`)
turned up 8 "broken links", all pre-existing (`diff` of the link lines between
HEAD and the worktree: LINKS_UNCHANGED_BY_MY_EDIT):

  sync/2026-09-18-curated-port.md: missing link target -> a.md#锚点
  sync/2026-09-18-curated-port.md: missing link target -> 目标
  sync/doc-claims-verification.md: dead anchor -> #minimal-and-fullscreen
  sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md: dead anchor -> #...
  sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md: missing link target -> ...
  sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md: missing link target -> ../../../../CHAOS.md
  sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md: missing link target -> 02-authentication.md
  sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md: missing link target -> NN-xxx.md#...

Seven of the eight were link syntax *inside inline code* -- the conventions doc
lists `](...)` and `](NN-xxx.md#...)` as things to watch for, and
`doc-claims-verification.md:190` quotes `](#minimal-and-fullscreen)` while
describing it as a dead anchor. `strip_code` blanks fenced blocks, so the tool
already understood "code is not a claim"; it just stopped at the fence boundary.

Fix: `mask_inline()` blanks backticked spans positionally (same character count,
so line/column reporting and every other mode are untouched) and `check_links`
consumes it. Deliberately NOT applied to the `--before/--after` invariants,
which compare inline spans *because* they are content.

Non-vacuity: new case in `check-doc-l10n-selftest.py`
(`links: inline-code syntax is an example, a prose link is still checked`) runs
the real `--links` twice over one throwaway directory -- examples-only file must
be clean, then adding a prose link must fail naming `prose.md`. Total is now
52/52. Mutation (delete the `mask_inline` call):

### MUTATION: --links ignores inline code spans again
FAIL links: inline-code syntax is an example, a prose link is still checked
RESTORED_BYTE_IDENTICAL

The eighth was a real, if small, defect: a blockquote in the conventions doc
quoting the authoritative config-root wording carried
`[CHAOS.md](../../../../CHAOS.md)`, while the shipped guides use five levels
(`rg "CHAOS\.md\)" crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/` -> `../../../../../` in
02/03/05/README). The quoted depth was a mis-transcription and resolved from
neither place; it now points at `../CHAOS.md`, which resolves from `sync/`.

`--english` on `sync/**` then had 6 of its 8 lines from one cause: the recon
records are *generated* by `scripts/upstream-recon.sh`, whose template was
English. Template rewritten in Chinese (headings, intro, table labels, next
steps) and the record regenerated against the live API:

$ bash scripts/upstream-recon.sh
upstream-recon: SOURCE_REV=72a61251f upstream=2bdd1d6a6 status=ahead ahead=9 behind=0
upstream-recon: wrote sync/recon/2026-10-02-2bdd1d6a6.md

Result on the previously unchecked glob:
  --links   : 0 broken link(s)          (was 8)
  --english : 2 lines                   (was 8)

The 2 that remain are `sync/doc-claims-verification.md:80` and `:287`, both
verbatim quotes of upstream English text inside 「…」 (a UI string
"…leaving the session" and a quoted PR statement). Quoting an upstream sentence
in its original language is the documented convention in
`sync/doc-l10n-conventions.md`, so they are left as decided literals, not
translated. `sync/**` is intentionally still not wired into CI -- it is
maintainer notes, not text compiled into the binary -- but it is now clean
enough that widening the check later is a one-line change rather than a cleanup
project.
